{5}-2\\sqrt{5}{\\sin }^{2}⁡{42}^{∘}$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186344970 1071 "Express $\\sqrt{5}-2\\sqrt{5}{sin}^{2}⁡{42}^{∘}$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186344970 1074 "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}(1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡{42}^{∘})\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={42}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡{42}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {42}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{84}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}(1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡{42}^{∘}) & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{84}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186344970 1074 "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡{42}^{∘})\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={42}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}⁡{42}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {42}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{84}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡{42}^{∘}) & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{84}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186344972 1071 "Find the exact value of ${\\cos }^{2}⁡({75}^{∘})-{\\sin }^{2}⁡({75}^{∘}).$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186344972 1071 "Find the exact value of ${cos}^{2}⁡({75}^{∘})-{sin}^{2}⁡({75}^{∘}).$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186344972 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={75}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cos }^{2}⁡({75}^{∘})-{\\sin }^{2}⁡({75}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {75}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186344972 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={75}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡({75}^{∘})-{sin}^{2}⁡({75}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {75}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186344974 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/7e/7eff12ed4bca73dc27f834b0740b5b2235440250e11427f911c8fc29c99fa1d7.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi <\\arg ⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  & =\\arctan ⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)≈0.927\\,30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\arg ⁡(z)≈\\pi +0.927\\,30≈4.07\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186344974 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/7e/7eff12ed4bca73dc27f834b0740b5b2235440250e11427f911c8fc29c99fa1d7.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi <arg⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  & =arctan⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)≈0.927\\,30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)≈\\pi +0.927\\,30≈4.07\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186344977 1071 "Find the exact value of $1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡({22.5}^{∘}).$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186344977 1071 "Find the exact value of $1-2{sin}^{2}⁡({22.5}^{∘}).$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186344977 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={22.5}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡({22.5}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {22.5}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186344977 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={22.5}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}⁡({22.5}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {22.5}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186344985 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {\\cos }^{2}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186344985 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {cos}^{2}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186344985 1074 "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta $ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡3x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {\\cos }^{2}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+\\frac{1}{2}x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+\\frac{x}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186344985 1074 "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x$, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡3x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {cos}^{2}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+\\frac{1}{2}x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+\\frac{x}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186345044 1074 "At the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$,  the vector ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,i+{\\sec }^{2}⁡t\\,j+\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,i+{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$  is  $\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k.$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186345044 1074 "At the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$,  the vector ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,i+{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,j+\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,i+{sec}^{2}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$  is  $\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k.$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186345062 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\theta ).$ Also, since $r=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dx}{d\\theta } & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\theta ))}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\csc }^{2}⁡(\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\csc }^{3}⁡(\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186345062 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\theta ).$ Also, since $r=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dx}{d\\theta } & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\theta ))}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡(\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186345176 1071 "Find the largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)={\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x+2}{3+{2}^{x}}\\right).$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186345176 1071 "Find the largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x+2}{3+{2}^{x}}\\right).$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186345176 1074 "Our function is a logarithmic function, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\nTo find the domain of ${\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x+2}{3+{2}^{x}}\\right),$ we set the expression under the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+2}{3+{2}^{x}}>0\\,⟹\\,2x+2>0\\,⟹\\,x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)={\\log }_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x+2}{3+{2}^{x}}\\right)$ is $(-1,\\infty ),$ and it is also continuous on this interval." 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186345176 1074 "Our function is a logarithmic function, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\nTo find the domain of ${log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x+2}{3+{2}^{x}}\\right),$ we set the expression under the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+2}{3+{2}^{x}}>0\\,⟹\\,2x+2>0\\,⟹\\,x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x+2}{3+{2}^{x}}\\right)$ is $(-1,\\infty ),$ and it is also continuous on this interval." 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186345177 1071 "Find the largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)={\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{{5}^{x}}\\right).$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186345177 1071 "Find the largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)={log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{{5}^{x}}\\right).$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186345177 1074 "Our function is a logarithmic function, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\nTo find the domain of ${\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{{5}^{x}}\\right),$ we set the expression under the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{{5}^{x}}>0\\,⟹\\,x-2>0\\,⟹\\,x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)={\\log }_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{{5}^{x}}\\right)$ is $(2,\\infty ),$ and it is also continuous on this interval." 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186345177 1074 "Our function is a logarithmic function, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\nTo find the domain of ${log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{{5}^{x}}\\right),$ we set the expression under the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{{5}^{x}}>0\\,⟹\\,x-2>0\\,⟹\\,x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)={log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{{5}^{x}}\\right)$ is $(2,\\infty ),$ and it is also continuous on this interval." 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186345208 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\,N-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nN-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (N-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-3\\cdot 3 & =0 \\\\ 2+2\\lambda +\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -7 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186345208 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\,N-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nN-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-3\\cdot 3 & =0 \\\\ 2+2\\lambda +\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -7 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186345209 1074 "Let's start by writing the matrix $N-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nN-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{7}-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $\\det (N-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (N-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{7}-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-\\sqrt{7}-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-0\\cdot 6 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda +\\sqrt{7}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda =-\\sqrt{7}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal." 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186345209 1074 "Let's start by writing the matrix $N-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nN-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{7}-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(N-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{7}-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-\\sqrt{7}-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-0\\cdot 6 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda +\\sqrt{7}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda =-\\sqrt{7}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal." 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186345214 1071 "Express $$\n\\displaystyle 3{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)-3{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\n$$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186345214 1071 "Express $$\n\\displaystyle 3{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)\n$$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186345214 1074 "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n3({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\