ing the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=3-4\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=3+4\\text{i}.$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(3-4\\text{i})+(3+4\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(3+3)+\\text{i}(-4+4) \\\\  & =6+\\text{i}0 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186351437 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/fc/fc6a2568ba77ce043ae0d48840ed4707b3e629b8a52c4b5f182c1e23a249fa44.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\arg ⁡(z)<\\pi .$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tan ⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\arctan ⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) \\\\  & ≈1.190\\text{ }29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\arg ⁡(z)≈\\pi -1.190\\text{ }29≈1.95\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186351437 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/fc/fc6a2568ba77ce043ae0d48840ed4707b3e629b8a52c4b5f182c1e23a249fa44.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<\\pi .$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) \\\\  & ≈1.190\\text{ }29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)≈\\pi -1.190\\text{ }29≈1.95\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186351439 1071 "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186351439 1071 "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186351439 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}={\\cos }^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=3-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186351439 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}={cos}^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=3-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186351441 1071 "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+4{\\sec }^{4}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true]