hen $|det(A)|=|det(B)|$” only." 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186354308 1074 "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 0 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ -7 & 0 & -5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -7 & -5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )+21) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +11) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the quadratic polynomial ${\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +11=0,$ we have that the discriminant $D<0.$ So, it doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable over $R.$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186354308 1074 "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 0 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ -7 & 0 & -5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -7 & -5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )+21) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +11) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the quadratic polynomial ${\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +11=0,$ we have that the discriminant $D<0.$ So, it doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable over $R.$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186354317 1074 "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|\\det (T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\det (T)| & =|9\\cdot 0-5\\cdot 7| \\\\  & =|0-35| \\\\  & =35.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186354317 1074 "The area scale 