se the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =4x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)={\\cos }^{2}⁡(4x)-{\\sin }^{2}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /16}({\\cos }^{2}⁡(4x)-{\\sin }^{2}⁡(4x))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{16})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186368397 1074 "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =4x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)={cos}^{2}⁡(4x)-{sin}^{2}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /16}({cos}^{2}⁡(4x)-{sin}^{2}⁡(4x))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{16})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186368410 1071 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨3\\sqrt{t},\\,{\\csc }^{2}⁡(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨4,\\,10⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the components of the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$\n\n$v(t)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186368410 1071 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨3\\sqrt{t},\\,{csc}^{2}⁡(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨4,\\,10⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the components of the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$\n\n$v(t)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186368410 1074 "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3\\sqrt{t},{\\csc }^{2}⁡(t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 3{t}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int {\\csc }^{2}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{3/2}+{C}_{1},-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨4,10⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨2(1)^{3/2}+{C}_{1},-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,10⟩ & =⟨2+{C}_{1},-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(1)+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(1)+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =10+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2t\\sqrt{t}+2,\\,-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)+10+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(1)⟩.\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186368410 1074 "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3\\sqrt{t},{csc}^{2}⁡(t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 3{t}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int {csc}^{2}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{3/2}+{C}_{1},-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨4,10⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨2(1)^{3/2}+{C}_{1},-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,10⟩ & =⟨2+{C}_{1},-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(1)+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2+{C}_{1}