\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot C)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=-2$ and $n=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(-2C) & =(-2)^{4}\\cdot det(C) \\\\  & =16\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-80.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186383202 1071 "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\det (A)=23.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}$ and ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $\\det (B)?$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186383202 1071 "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(A)=23.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}$ and ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186383202 1074 "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5{R}_{3}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we only have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (B) & =-\\det (A) \\\\  & =-(23) \\\\  & =-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186383202 1074 "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5{R}_{3}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we only have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(23) \\\\  & =-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186383209 1071 "Suppose $U$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\det (U)=21.$ The matrix $V$ is obtained from $U$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-7){R}_{4}$ and ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $\\det (V)?$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186383209 1071 "Suppose $U$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(U)=21.$ The matrix $V$ is obtained from $U$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-7){R}_{4}$ and ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(V)?$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186383209 1074 "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-7){R}_{4}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{1}{3},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{1}{3}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $\\frac{1}{3}.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (V) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\det (U) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 21 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186383209 1074 "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-7){R}_{4}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{1}{3},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{1}{3}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $\\frac{1}{3}.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(V) & =\\frac{1}{3}det(U) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 21 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186383216 1071 "Given that $\\det (A)=\\det (B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186383216 1071 "Given that $det(A)=det(B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186383216 1074 "Since $\\det (A)=\\det (B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{3} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\det (B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{3}.\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186383216 1074 "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{3} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n