6411 true] [17592186385222 1071 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y(t)^{2}+1) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=3y(t)+{tan}^{-1}⁡(x(t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186385222 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1) \\\\ 3y+{\\tan }^{-1}⁡(x)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1))=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1))=\\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3y+{\\tan }^{-1}⁡(x))=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3y+{\\tan }^{-1}⁡(x))=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+(0)^{2}} & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=2,\\text{ }3.$\n\nFinally, since we both have two real and positive eigenvalues, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{source}.$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186385222 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1) \\\\ 3y+{tan}^{-1}⁡(x)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1))=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1))=\\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3y+{tan}^{-1}⁡(x))=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3y+{tan}^{-1}⁡(x))=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+(0)^{2}} & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is 