}$” is *not*  an injection.  For example, $g(1)=1=g(-1),$ but $1\\ne -1.$\n\n- “$h:[0,2\\pi ]\\to R$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$” is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=1=h(2\\pi ),$ but $0\\ne 2\\pi .$\n\n- “$f:Q\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={2}^{x}$” is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡({2}^{{x}_{1}}) & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{{x}_{2}}) \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f:Q\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={2}^{x}$” only." 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186385913 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3},$ and the first partial derivatives exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ defined by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{curl} \\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\times \\mathbf{F}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 0 & xy{e}^{z} & yz{e}^{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz{e}^{x})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy{e}^{z})]\\mathbf{i}-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz{e}^{x})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(0)]\\mathbf{j}+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{z})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(0)]\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(z{e}^{x}-xy{e}^{z})\\mathbf{i}-(yz{e}^{x}-0)\\mathbf{j}+(y{e}^{z}-0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(z{e}^{x}-xy{e}^{z})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-yz{e}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+y{e}^{z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(0,2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F})(0,2,1) & =(1\\cdot {e}^{0}-0\\cdot 2\\cdot {e}^{1})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\cdot {e}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+2e\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186385913 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3},$ and the first partial derivatives exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ defined by\n\n$$\ncurl\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\times \\mathbf{F}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 0 & xy{e}^{z} & yz{e}^{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz{e}^{x})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy{e}^{z})]\\mathbf{i}-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz{e}^{x})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(0)]\\mathbf{j}+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{z})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(0)]\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(z{e}^{x}-xy{e}^{z})\\mathbf{i}-(yz{e}^{x}-0)\\mathbf{j}+(y{e}^{z}-0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(z{e}^{x}-xy{e}^{z})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-yz{e}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+y{e}^{z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(0,2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F})(0,2,1) & =(1\\cdot {e}^{0}-0\\cdot 2\\cdot {e}^{1})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\cdot {e}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+2e\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186385920 1074 "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{2}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{3}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{2x} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 4{e}^{2x} & -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ 2 & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 4 & -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}[(8{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2x)+8{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2x))-2(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+4(2{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2x)+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2x))] \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}[8-2(0)+4(2)] \\\\  & =16{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{2},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{2}(x) & =\\begin{vmatrix