erical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.6) & =2(0.6)^{4}-(0.6)^{5} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.129\\,6-1\\cdot 0.077\\,76 \\\\  & =0.259\\,2-0.077\\,76 \\\\  & =0.181\\,44 \\\\  & ≈0.181.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =2(0.2)^{4}-(0.2)^{5} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.001\\,6-1\\cdot 0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.003\\,2-0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.002\\,88 \\\\  & ≈0.003.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.2}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={3}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.181\\,44$ to $0.002\\,88.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.181\\,44}{0.002\\,88}=63≈63.000.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈63 \\\\ p & ≈{\\log }_{3}⁡(63) \\\\ p & ≈3.771….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=2(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,e(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186391487 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive intege