ing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(4-15)+3(-2+9)+1(-5+6) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-11)+3\\cdot 7+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-22+21+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\det (D)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$D$ is a singular matrix” and $\\det (D)=0$ are true." 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186400352 1074 "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(4-15)+3(-2+9)+1(-5+6) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-11)+3\\cdot 7+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-22+21+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(D)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$D$ is a singular matrix” and $det(D)=0$ are true." 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186400359 1074 "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (S) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2\\sqrt{7} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{7} & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & \\sqrt{7}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{7}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{7}\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{7} 