n element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- “$f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=-{\\log }_{2}⁡(x)$” is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in R,$ we can choose $x={2}^{-y}\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Then $f(x)=f({2}^{-y})=-{\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{-y})=y.$\n\n- “$h:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={3}^{x}$” is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ we can choose $x={\\log }_{3}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $h(x)=h({\\log }_{3}⁡y)={3}^{{\\log }_{3}⁡(y)}=y.$\n\n- “$g:Q\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$” is an into function. Let's take $y=\\sqrt[3]{(\\sqrt{2})}\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}=y.$ Indeed, in this case we would have \n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x}=y\\,⇔\\,\\sqrt[3]{x}=\\sqrt[3]{(\\sqrt{2})}\\,⇔\\,x=\\sqrt{2}\\in Q,\n$$\n\n which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$g:Q\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$” only." 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186410014 1074 "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- “$f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=-{log}_{2}⁡(x)$” is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in R,$ we can choose $x={2}^{-y}\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Then $f(x)=f({2}^{-y})=-{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{-y})=y.$\n\n- “$h:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={3}^{x}$” is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ we can choose $x={log}_{3}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $h(x)=h({log}_{3}⁡y)={3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(y)}=y.$\n\n- “$g:Q\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$” is an into function. Let's take $y=\\sqrt[3]{(\\sqrt{2})}\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=\\sq