name{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ &  \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nsince $t>0.$ \n\nHence, the corresponding unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the curvature for  $t>0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186411868 1074 "Let $T(t)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the parameter t. Since we are given the curve r(t) parametrized by the parameter \nt (not the arc length s), we will use the following formula for the curvature\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ &  \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nsince $t>0.$ \n\nHence, the corresponding unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the curvature for  $t>0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186411963 1071 "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $A$ and $B$ are row equivalent matrices and $\\det (A)=1$, find $\\det (B).$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 12 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186411963 1071 "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $A$ and $B$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(A)=1$, find $det(B).$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 12 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186411963 1074 "Notice that $B$ can be obtained from $A$ by multiplying the $2$nd row by $4.$ \n\nSo, the determinant is multiplied by $4$ as well.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (B) & =4\\cdot \\det (A) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186411963 1074 "Notice that $B$ can be obtained from $A$ by multiplying the $2$nd row by $4.$ \n\nSo, the determinant is multiplied by $4$ as well.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =4\\cdot det(A) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186411964 1071 "Suppose $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $\\det (A)=-31.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{4}$ (switching the second and the fourth rows). What is the value of $\\det (B)?$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186411964 1071 "Suppose $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=-31.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{4}$ (switching the second and the fourth rows). What is the value of $det(B)?$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186411964 1074 "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (B) & =-\\det (A) \\\\  & =-(-31) \\\\  & =31.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186411964 1074 "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(-31) \\\\  & =31.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186411965 1071 "Suppose $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\det (B)=12.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}↔{R}_{2}$ (switching the second and the third rows). What is the value of $\\det (C)?$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186411965 1071 "Suppose $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $det(B)=12.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}↔{R}_{2}$ (switching the second and the third rows). What is the value of $det(C)?$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186411965 1074 "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (C) & =-\\det (B) \\\\  & =-(12) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186411965 1074 "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =-det(B) \\\\  & =-(12) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186411966 1071 "Suppose $C$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $\\det (C)=-24.$ The matrix $D$ is obtained from $C$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{5}$ and ${R}_{3}:=-\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $\\det (D)?$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186411966 1071 "Suppose $C$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(C)=-24.$ The matrix $D$ is obtained from $C$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{5}$ and ${R}_{3}:=-\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(D)?$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186411966 1074 "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{5}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:=-\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{3}$ involves multiplying a row by $-\\frac{1}{6},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $-\\frac{1}{6}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $-\\frac{1}{6}$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (D) & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-\\det (C)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\det (C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-24) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186411966 1074 "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{5}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:=-\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{3}$ involves multiplying a row by $-\\frac{1}{6},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $-\\frac{1}{6}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $-\\frac{1}{6}$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-det(C)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}det(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-24) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186411967 1071 "Suppose $B$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $\\det (B)=103.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{7}↔{R}_{3}$ (switching the seventh and the third rows). What is the value of $\\det (C)?$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186411967 1071 "Suppose $B$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $det(B)=103.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{7}↔{R}_{3}$ (switching the seventh and the third rows). What is the value of $det(C)?$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186411967 1074 "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (C) & =-\\det (B) \\\\  & =-(103) \\\\  & =-103.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186411967 1074 "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =-det(B) \\\\  & =-(103) \\\\  & =-103.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186411968 1071 "Suppose $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\det (B)=102.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}.$ What is the value of $\\det (C)?$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186411968 1071 "Suppose $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $det(B)=102.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}.$ What is the value of $det(C)?$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186411968 1074 "The row operation applied to $B$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (C) & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\det (B) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 102 \\\\  & =-51.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186411968 1074 "The row operation applied to $B$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =-\\frac{1}{2}det(B) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 102 \\\\  & =-51.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186411969 1071 "Suppose $X$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\det (X)=-60.$ The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $\\det (Y)?$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186411969 1071 "Suppose $X$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $det(X)=-60.$ The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(Y)?$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186411969 1074 "The row operation applied to $X$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (Y) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\det (X) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-60) \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186411969 1074 "The row operation applied to $X$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =\\frac{1}{4}det(X) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-60) \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186411970 1071 "Suppose $B$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $\\det (B)=120.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $\\det (C)?$" 