n we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =-\\frac{2}{23}det(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{23}\\cdot (-46) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186412061 1071 "Given that $\\det (A)=14$, and $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186412061 1071 "Given that $det(A)=14$, and $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186412061 1074 "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})=\\text{det}(A)=14\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\det (I)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3I & =3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a diagonal matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & ={3}^{3}=27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}(3I)=-13$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}(3I)=14-27=-13\n$$\n\n- Statement $-\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(I)=-13$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n-\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(I)=-14+1=-13\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=-28$ is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=14-14=0\\ne -28\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}(3I)=-13$; $-\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(I)=-13$ only." 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186412061 1074 "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})=\\text{det}(A)=14\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(