tial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $H(1,1)>0$ is false. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=\\det ({f}^{″}(1,1))=2\\cdot 0-{1}^{2}=-1<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0$ is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=2>0.$\n\n- Statement “The point $(1,1)$ is a local minimum of $f$” is false. Since $H(1,1)<0,$ then $(1,1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0$ only." 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186420504 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $H(1,1)>0$ is false. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))=2\\cdot 0-{1}^{2}=-1<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0$ is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=2>0.$\n\n- Statement “The point $(1,1)$ is a local minimum of $f$” is false. Since $H(1,1)<0,$ then $(1,1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0$ only." 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186420693 1071 "$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)={\\sec }^{2}⁡x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186420693 1071 "$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186420693 1074 "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={\\sec }^{2}⁡x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={\\sec }^{2}⁡x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the solution to the differential equation ${f}^{′}(x)={\\sec }^{2}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C\\in R$ is the arbitrary constant.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition “$f$ is a differentiable function” is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={\\sec }^{2}⁡x)\\,⇍\\,(f\\text{ is a differentiable function})\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the function $f(x)={e}^{x}$ is a differentiable function but ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne {\\sec }^{2}⁡x.$\n\n- Condition $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={\\sec }^{2}⁡x)\\,⇏\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, for the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1,$ the statement ${f}^{′}(x)={\\sec }^{2}⁡x$ is true but $f(x)\\ne \\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1.$\n\n- Condition “$f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C,$ for any $C\\in R$” is both necessary and sufficient: The statement ${f}^{′}(x)={\\sec }^{2}⁡x$ is true *if and only if* $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C,$ for any $C\\in R.$\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={\\sec }^{2}⁡x)\\,⇒\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={\\sec }^{2}⁡x)\\,⇐\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={\\sec }^{2}⁡x)\\,⇔\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C,$ for any $C\\in R$” only." 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186420693 1074 "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the solution to the differential equation ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C\\in R$ is the arbitrary constant.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition “$f$ is a differentiable function” is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇍\\,(f\\text{ is a differentiable function})\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the function $f(x)={e}^{x}$ is a differentiable function but ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne {sec}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n- Condition $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇏\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, for the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1,$ the statement ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ is true but $f(x)\\ne \\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1.$\n\n- Condition “$f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C,$ for any $C\\in R$” is both necessary and sufficient: The statement ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ is true *if and only if* $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C,$ for any $C\\in R.$\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇒\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇐\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇔\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C,$ for any $C\\in R$” only." 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186420847 1071 "Find $\\det ({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186420847 1071 "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186420847 1074 "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\det ({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\det (A):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\det \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =20-18 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\det ({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186420847 1074 "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =20-18 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186420848 1071 "Find $\\det ({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 true] [17592186420848 1071 "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139545674 17592186576411 false] [17592186420848 1074 "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\det ({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\det (A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (A) & =\\det \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =6-2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we o